## 5. Common Mistakes

### Using `catch unreachable` for errors that can happen

```zig
const val = mayFail() catch unreachable; // BAD if failure is possible
```

`unreachable` is for truly impossible situations, like violating an internal invariant. In safe build modes, reaching it triggers a panic. Overuse masks real bugs. Only use it when you can mathematically prove the error set is empty.

### Ignoring error set inference and returning `anyerror`

```zig
fn doThing() anyerror!void { … }
```

`anyerror` is the global error set—every error defined anywhere. It defeats the compiler’s ability to narrow down possible failures. Callers cannot know what errors to handle without reading the implementation. Always prefer specific or inferred sets.

### Forgetting that error sets merge upward

```zig
fn validate(path: []const u8) !void {
    const file = std.fs.cwd().openFile(path, .{}); // inferred error includes FileNotFound, etc.
    // This function's error set suddenly includes file system errors,
    // even if the function's name suggests only validation errors.
}
```

Callers of `validate` will see all those low-level errors. Consider wrapping them into a higher-level abstraction:

```zig
const file = std.fs.cwd().openFile(path, .{}) catch |err| {
    if (err == error.FileNotFound) return error.ConfigMissing;
    return err;
};
```

Now the caller sees only `ConfigMissing`.

### Trying to assign an error union directly to a variable of payload type

```zig
var x: u32 = mayFail(); // compile error: cannot coerce !u32 to u32
```

The compiler demands handling. Use `catch`, `try`, or `if` to extract the payload.

### Misusing `if (err)` instead of destructuring

```zig
const result = mayFail();
if (result) |val| { … }
else |err| { … }
```

Not to be confused with `if (maybeError) |err|`—which would be wrong. The destructuring syntax is unambiguous, but newcomers from Go might mistakenly write `if err != null` patterns. Zig uses the union’s structure directly.

